(one further trial, same result: exactly 1 POST)

Tally across 4 valid back-to-back double-tap trials on the identical Retry coordinate:
2 produced a DUPLICATE POST, 2 produced a single POST (~50% reproduction rate).

Root cause (source-read, packages/nears_dls/lib/props/n_element.dart +
UserApp/lib/features/interest/screens/interest_screen.dart +
UserApp/lib/features/category/controllers/category_controller.dart):
NErrorPanel's `isDisabled: categoryController.isLoading` gate is applied via
`wrapUniversal()` -> `IgnorePointer`, and `CategoryController.saveInterest()` sets
`_isLoading = true; update();` SYNCHRONOUSLY before its first `await`. Both the
`_saveInterestError = null` setState (interest_screen.dart's `_saveInterests`) and
the GetX `update()` call mark the tree dirty, but Flutter only rebuilds+re-lays-out
(so the IgnorePointer/removed-button actually takes effect for hit-testing) on the
NEXT FRAME. Two raw touch-up events delivered by the OS within that one frame's
window (observed ~8ms apart, well under one 16ms frame at 60Hz) can BOTH be
dispatched to the pre-rebuild tree, so both reach `onRetry`/`onPressed` before the
guard has visually/hit-test-wise taken effect -- a genuine frame-timing race, not
something the widget-test suite's `tester.tap()` -> `tester.pump()` -> `tester.tap()`
sequencing can ever exercise (pump() forces the intervening rebuild the real device
does not guarantee before the second raw input arrives).

Impact: wasted duplicate backend call on every affected retry (not currently data-
corrupting for the plain double-tap-same-button case, since both taps carry the same
selection snapshot -- but the QA Test Scope's own cross-check scenario, "change
selection between the failure and retry", means a sufficiently-fast double-tap
straddling a selection edit is not provably safe from this same race).
